---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

// These question bodies are hand-written HTML, so their hrefs need the site
// base prefixed the same way u() does it. Both fields carry links - applying it
// to only one of them left five links broken in the built site.
const base = (html) => html.replace(/href='\//g, `href='${u("/")}`);

const Q = [
  { n: 1, w: "answered", t: "Who were Ljubomir's parents? — ANSWERED",
    b: "This was the archive's central doubt: the join at generation three rested on a man with no document. The <a href='/marriage-1920'><strong>Senj marriage register of 8 December 1920</strong></a> names the groom's parents outright — <strong>Juraj Blažević and Tereza <em>r.</em> Žubrinić</strong> — and confirms Ljubomir's birth as 1 March 1892. It was attached in the family tree the whole time and absent from the GEDCOM export.",
    f: "Nothing further. <strong>What replaces it is question 1b.</strong>" },
  { n: "1b", w: "high", t: "Who was Juraj Blažević's father? — a house number, and a second witness",
    b: "Four more registers were read in September 2026 and the man is no longer a bare name.<br><br><strong>He has an occupation and a house.</strong> His son <strong>Marijan's baptism, 12 April 1895</strong>, enters him as <strong>“Jurko Blažević i Tereza rođ. Žubrinić, <em>selj.</em>”</strong> — peasant — living at <strong>Smokvica kbr. 114</strong>.<br><br><strong>That house is a zadruga, not a household — this archive's own correction.</strong> <a href='/people/toma-tomislav-blazevic'>Toma</a> and Terezija Pećanić are recorded at <strong>Smokvica 114</strong> in <strong>1882</strong> and <strong>1884</strong>, and Toma was born at <em>Br. 114</em> in 1838. But a control document — the 1890 baptism of Frane Blažević — puts <strong>Pavao Blažević, son of Luka</strong>, at Smokvica 114 as well. Luka and Toma's father Anton are both sons of <a href='/people/ilija-blazevic'>Ilija</a>, so Pavao and Toma are first cousins, and the number covers an <strong>extended-family compound</strong>. It places Juraj in Ilija's kin-group; it cannot name his father. <a href='/blazevic'>The zadruga →</a><br><br><strong>And a second register names the couple.</strong> <a href='/people/anka-blazevic'>Anka Blažević's</a> marriage to Pavao Kosina on <strong>20 November 1919</strong> gives her parents as <strong>Juraj Blažević i Tereza r. Žubrinić</strong> — independently of the 1920 entry for their son Ljubomir.<br><br><strong>And the house is now written out in full.</strong> <a href='/people/antonija-blazevic-2'>Antonija “Tonka” Blažević's</a> marriage to Ginro Babić on <strong>1 December 1912</strong> enters her parents as <strong>“††Toma i Tereza Blažević, poljodjelci <em>iz sela Smokvica kbr. 114</em>”</strong> — not a number in a baptismal margin but the household stated in plain Croatian, both of them marked dead. That is a <strong>fourth independent document</strong> for house 114 and the first to name Toma and Tereza together with the number. It hardens the household the whole argument stands on; it still does not name Juraj's father.",
    f: "<strong>And the godparents close the circle.</strong> The tree gives Juraj two younger siblings, <a href='/people/marija-blazevic-2'>Marija (b. 1880)</a> and <a href='/people/ivan-blazevic'>Ivan (b. 1882)</a> — children of Toma at house 114. The godparents at Marijan's 1895 baptism are entered as <strong>“Ive Blažević, <em>sin Tomin</em>”</strong> and <strong>“Marija Blažević, <em>ćer Tomina</em>”</strong>: the son and the daughter of a Toma, of the right names and the right ages, standing for the child of the man who lives in Toma's house. With the house number demoted to a kin-group, <strong>the godparents carry the weight</strong> — and they carry it well. Godparents are <em>chosen</em>, and a man's own brother and sister are the classic choice.<br><br><strong>September 2026 — and the patronymic turns out to be deliberate.</strong> Reading the 1888 baptism of Pavao's daughter Terezija, also at Smokvica 114, gives a godfather called <strong>Ivo Blažević <em>sin Matin</em></strong> — son of <em>Mate</em>. So <strong>two different men named Ivo Blažević stood godfather at this one house within seven years</strong>, told apart only by their fathers' names. That is why the priest bothered with a patronymic at all: there were several Ivo Blaževići about, and <em>“sin Tomin”</em> in 1895 is a distinction he chose to draw, not a formula. It also shows the compound from the outside — one house number holding Toma's children, Pavao's children, Jurko's children, an Ivo son of Toma and an Ivo son of Mate.<br><br><strong>September 2026 — and the godparent column says it plainly.</strong> Four baptisms at Smokvica 114 have now been read, and the godparents are drawn from <strong>two distinct Blažević sibling-sets</strong>:<br><br><span class='mono'>1888 &nbsp;Ivo Blažević <em>sin Matin</em> · Anica Tomljanović<br>1894 &nbsp;Šime <strong>Pećanić</strong> · Mara Blažević <em>Matina</em><br>1895 &nbsp;Ive Blažević <em>sin Tomin</em> · Marija Blažević <em>ćer Tomina</em><br>1896 &nbsp;Nikola Blažević · Ana Krpan <em>kći Ilijina</em></span><br><br>Mate's children and Toma's children, both standing at the same house, plus a <strong>Pećanić</strong> — Toma's wife's family. <strong>That is what a zadruga looks like from the godparent column</strong>: one number, several fathers, and the children of one brother standing for the children of another. It still does not name Jurko's father; it does make the compound something you can see rather than infer.<br><br>It is still not the sentence that says so. What would give it: <strong>Jurko's own baptism</strong> in the Sv. Jakov Krmpote register, or his <strong>marriage to Tereza Žubrinić</strong> before 1892. Two of his children, <strong>Marijan (1895)</strong> and <strong>Ruža (1897)</strong>, have no marriage recorded anywhere — and every marriage entry re-names the parents, so those are two more chances at the same answer." },
  { n: "1c", w: "answered", t: "Who was Milka's first husband? — ANSWERED",
    b: "<strong>Zvonimir M. Vukelić</strong>, <em>gostioničarski pomoćnik</em> — innkeeper's assistant — born 13 January 1883, married at Senj on <strong>4 November 1907</strong> (marriages p.294 no.30). He <strong>died in 1915</strong>, aged thirty-two, leaving Milka a widow at twenty-eight. Their children <strong>Miroslava “Mica” Vukelić (1907–1993)</strong> and <strong>Josip Vukelić (1913–2004)</strong> are <strong>Hedviga's half-sister and half-brother</strong> — and were in the tree all along. <a href='/milka'>Milka's life →</a>",
    f: "Nothing further on identity. <strong>What remains is 1d.</strong>" },
  { n: "1d", w: "medium", t: "How did Zvonimir Vukelić die in 1915? — and a correction",
    b: "This archive proposed the Austro-Hungarian casualty lists, reasoning that he was thirty-two in the second year of the war. <strong>That reasoning was probably wrong.</strong> The tree places his death <strong>at Senj</strong>, in his own town, and gives his occupation as <strong><em>gostioničar, bankar</em></strong> — innkeeper and <strong>banker</strong>, a considerable rise from the <em>gostioničarski pomoćnik</em> who married Milka in 1907. A man who dies at home, in a settled trade, is not a <em>Verlustliste</em> entry.<br><br>His baptism has since been read: born <strong>13 January 1883</strong> at Senj, baptised 25 February, son of <strong>Petar <em>pl.</em> Vukelić</strong> — <em>plemeniti</em>, <strong>noble</strong> — and Lojza. Milka married into a titled family and was widowed out of it at twenty-eight.",
    f: "<strong>And a third occupation, from a third document.</strong> On 16 August 1914 he stands witness at the wedding of Milka's sister Franjka, entered as <strong>“Zvonko M. Vukelić, <em>pivar</em>”</strong> — brewer. The trajectory now reads <em>gostioničarski pomoćnik</em> (1907) → <em>pivar</em> (1914) → <em>gostioničar, bankar</em> (at death). It is also the first document found that places him inside this family other than his own marriage entry.<br><br>The <strong>Senj death register for 1915</strong>, and nothing else. The casualty lists are behind a subscription for uncommon surnames and are, on this evidence, the wrong book." },
  { n: "1e", w: "medium", t: "Which came first — the wedding or the child?",
    b: "Milka and Zvonimir married on 4 November 1907 <strong>by episcopal dispensation, with the banns read once on 3 November</strong> rather than three times. Their daughter Miroslava was born in <strong>1907</strong>. A dispensation, a one-day wedding and a child in the same year make a familiar shape — but the register does not date the birth, and this archive does not claim to know.<br><br><strong>Weakened 13 September 2026.</strong> The Senj marriage of <strong>Martin Kosina and Angjelika Boras, 12 January 1888</strong>, carries the <em>same</em> shape: dispensed by the Senj ordinariate on 7 January, banns read <strong>once only</strong> on the 8th, married on the 12th. He was <strong>47</strong> and she <strong>30</strong>, and there is no suggestion of anything in it. <strong>A single dispensation in this diocese looks like ordinary administrative practice, not evidence</strong> — so this question rests on the birth date alone, and that is exactly what is missing.",
    f: "<strong>Miroslava Vukelić's baptismal entry, Senj 1907</strong>, which gives the day." },
  { n: 2, w: "critical", t: "How did Oto Blažević die?",
    b: "Two accounts in the same note, from two named relatives: <strong>a snakebite</strong>, and <strong>killed in the war in Slavonia with no known grave</strong>. A photograph of <a href='/grave'>the family grave</a> now corroborates the second witness in its checkable parts — Oto's name is on the stone, and Leo and Katica were added later exactly as she described. It still does not establish the <em>cause</em>. <a href='/oto'>The full page →</a>",
    f: "A search of the <strong>Yugoslav war victims register</strong> (<em>Popis žrtava rata</em>) and Croatian State Archives military death records for <strong>Oto Blažević, b. 1923, Senj</strong>. An entry — or a confirmed absence — settles it." },
  { n: 3, w: "high", t: "When did Santou Codron leave Rhodes?",
    b: "Born on Rhodes in <strong>1913</strong>; alive in South Africa to marry Hedviga about <strong>1973</strong>. The island's Jewish community was deported on 23 July 1944 and roughly 150 of 1,615 survived. Whether he left before the war — as ~2,000 Rhodes Jews did, for the Congo and Rhodesia — or survived the deportation is <strong>not known here</strong>, and the difference is enormous. <a href='/rhodes'>The full page →</a>",
    f: "South African <strong>immigration and naturalisation papers</strong> giving arrival year and last residence; the <strong>Rhodes Jewish Museum</strong> community registers and <strong>Yad Vashem</strong> deportation lists, searched for Codron." },
  { n: 4, w: "critical", t: "Is the Port Pirie branch the same Žubrinić family?",
    b: "Now traceable three generations back from the emigrant: <a href='/people/joannes-juan-ivan-ive-john-zubrinic'>Ivan Žubrinić</a> (1860–1930) → his father <strong>Joannes, b. 19 June 1837, Forest Ranger</strong> → <strong>Elias “Ilija” (1806–1845)</strong> → <strong>Casparus (1767–1829)</strong>. And there it stops: in the tree, Casparus's parent is a node literally named <em class='mono'>“Brothers of Casparus (It Seems) Žubrinić”</em> — a researcher's guess written into the structure. <strong>Nothing joins the two lines</strong> — and as of 13 September 2026 it is possible to say exactly what the <em>apparent</em> join is made of. Walk Casparus upward and the chain reads: <span class='mono'>Casparus 1767 → “Brothers of Casparus (It Seems)” → “C Žubrinić Xubrinich Zubrinic” → “Žubrinić Zubrinix Xubrinic Zubrinic Otocac”</span>. The second is a researcher's note. <strong>The third is an alphabet</strong> — one of eighteen nodes whose entire given name is a single letter, holding everyone whose name starts with it. <a href='/zubrinic-roots'>Ninety-two people are attached that way.</a><br><br>Walk <a href='/people/toma-zubrinic'>Toma</a> upward and he stops at a bare node called <span class='mono'>Žubrinić</span> after one step. So the two lines meet in a filing system and nowhere else.<br><br><strong>But they are born a year apart in one small village.</strong> Casparus 14 January 1767; Toma 1768, at <strong>Šumećica 2</strong> — and the 1834 Frontier baptism this archive has read is at <strong>Šumećica 24</strong>. A surname held by ~312 people on earth, two men born a year apart in the same hamlet. <a href='/frontier'>The Frontier →</a>",
    f: "The <strong>Otočac parish registers for the 1760s</strong> — the baptisms of Casparus (14 January 1767) and Toma (1768), two entries a few pages apart, which would name one father or two." },
  { n: "4b", w: "medium", t: "When did Elias Žubrinić and Ana Untersinger actually die?",
    b: "This question used to read <em>“what killed them in 1845?”</em>, on the reasoning that two adults dying in one year suggests an epidemic or a famine in 1840s Lika. <strong>Tested on 13 September 2026, that did not survive.</strong><br><br>Across the whole 15,643-person export, 1845 carries <strong>27 deaths against a mean of 25.8</strong> for 1820–1880 — no spike whatever. Restricted to deaths at a <em>Croatian</em> place it carries <strong>none at all</strong>, and the worst Croatian years in this archive are 1875, 1880 and 1867.<br><br>The explanation is in how the dates are written. <strong>Six people in this family carry the bare string <span class='mono'>1845</span></strong> — no day, no month, no place, no source: Elias, Ana Untersinger, Phillipus Žubrinić, Martinus Žubrinić, a Maria born 1838, a Maria born 1809, and Nicolaus Kraljić. The only two 1845 deaths in the whole archive that carry a <em>real</em> date — Jelena Katnić on 21 November, Mihovil Kalanj on 13 March — belong to other families entirely.<br><br><strong>An epidemic entered in a register produces days and months. A year applied in bulk produces exactly this.</strong> So 1845 is far better read as somebody's estimate for a group who dropped out of the record together — which also dissolves three of the <a href='/impossible'>contradictions</a>, because children born in 1847 are not impossible if the parents' death year is a guess.",
    f: "<strong>Any decade of the Otočac death register between 1830 and 1860.</strong> Not to find an epidemic — to find out when these six actually died. On <strong>Untersinger</strong>, a German surname in Lika which the Frontier's administrative class would explain, see <a href='/connected'>the connected families</a>." },
  { n: "4d", w: "answered", t: "Which Maria Orešković, and was there a second wife? — NO SECOND WIFE, THREE MEN",
    b: "<strong>Answered 13 September 2026, from the tree's own structure.</strong> With the surname fold fixed — it had been excluding 63 Žubrinići — the question comes apart cleanly.<br><br><strong>Ahnentafel 20's children are a coherent set:</strong> <span class='mono'>Nicolaus 1834 · Daniel 1837 · Francisca 1840 · Maria 1841</span>. Four children in seven years at Otočac. That is one marriage, contracted about 1833, behaving exactly as a marriage should.<br><br><strong>And the tree holds a second Michael</strong> — Michael Miho Michaël Žubrinić, married to <strong>Maria Marković</strong>, whose children are <span class='mono'>Theodorus 1841 · <strong>Josephus, 16 March 1845</strong> · Marta 1849 · Magdalena 1852</span>.<br><br><strong>That is the entry this archive read.</strong> The Otočac baptism of 16 March 1845 — <em>Michaël et Maria Žubrinić nata Marković</em>, the one that made this question suspect a second wife — is <em>his</em>, and the tree has held him all along.<br><br><strong>What went wrong is a filing error.</strong> The 1845 scan is tagged to ahnentafel 20. This archive read it, found a wife <em>née Marković</em> where it expected <em>née Orešković</em>, and invented a second marriage to explain the difference. There was no second marriage. There are at least <strong>three</strong> men of the name — the third being the <em>††Mate Žubrinić i Marija rodj. Orešković</em> named at the 1910 marriage as parents of <a href='/people/nikola-zubrinic-xubrinic'>Nikola, b. 1880</a>, who is also wrongly hung on ahnentafel 20, thirty-nine years after that coherent set ends.",
    f: "<strong>Nothing further on the second wife — there was none.</strong> What remains is smaller and still worth doing: <strong>which</strong> of the three couples <a href='/people/maria-catharina-oreskovic-oreskovic'>Maria Catharina Orešković</a>, born 8 September 1835, actually belongs to. She cannot be the mother of children born 1834 to 1841, so she is the third couple's, and her whole Orešković–Sekula ancestry moves with her.<br><br>The <strong>Otočac marriage register around 1855–70</strong> would name that couple outright. And a small tidy: <strong>Josephus 1845 is in the tree twice</strong>, both entries dated 16 March 1845 — one baptism recorded as two people." },
  { n: "4c", w: "medium", t: "Can the Žubrinići be joined into one family? — NO, NOT FROM HERE",
    b: "<strong>Both numbers this question was built on were wrong, and the answer changes with them.</strong> The tree holds <strong>422</strong> people of the name, not 232 — the surname fold split on a bracket but not a slash, so <span class='mono'>Žubrinić / Xubrinich</span> never matched. And they resolve into <strong>90</strong> loose ends, which is high for a reason the old figure never named: <strong>92 people hang off an A–Z index</strong>, eighteen nodes whose whole given name is a single letter.<br><br><strong>Of the 90 loose ends, 79 are bare names</strong> — no date, no place, nothing to search on. Four have both. So joining them is not the largest piece of work outstanding here; it is <strong>work that cannot be done from this archive at all</strong>, and saying otherwise was the mistake. <a href='/zubrinic-roots'>The fragments, counted →</a>",
    f: "A systematic read of the <strong>Otočac registers</strong> (and Prozor, Šumećica, Kompolje), rather than record-by-record hint-following. This is a job for someone who can work in Latin parish hands and knows that <strong>X spells Ž</strong> before about 1850." },
  { n: 5, w: "answered", t: "Are Antić and Papić one family or two? — ONE, and a by-name explains it",
    b: "<strong>The tree's own structure answers most of this.</strong> Of 15,643 people there are <strong>7 Antić and 117 Papić</strong>, and they <em>never interleave</em>: <strong>no family anywhere contains both an Antić and a Papić child</strong>. The two names touch at exactly one point — <a href='/people/georgius-juraj-joannis-papa-antic-papic'>Georgius Juraj “Jure” Antić Papić</a>, ahnentafel 12, born at Selce on 11 March 1838, who is entered carrying <strong>both surnames and the word <em>Papa</em></strong>. Every one of the nine cross-name parent-child links in the tree is a child of his. Everything above him is Antić; everything below is Papić.<br><br>So this was never a question about two families. It is a question about <strong>one join</strong> — and the register says what happened at it.<br><br><strong>Ahnentafel 24's baptism, Vrbnik, 14 March 1806:</strong> <em class='mono'>patre Joanne, Matre Marthæ Antich <u>Papa</u>, nomine Josephum</em>. The family is <strong>Antić</strong>. <strong><em>Papa</em> is its house by-name</strong> — underlined by the scribe exactly as he underlines <em>Loncsarich <u>Franich</u></em> and <em>Antich <u>Dudin</u></em> on the same page. A <strong>nadimak</strong>, the device that tells one household from another where a village shares a surname, doing the same work as the <a href='/gazetteer'>house numbers</a> at Smokvica.<br><br><span class='mono'>1806 &nbsp;Antich Papa — surname Antić, by-name Papa<br>1838 &nbsp;ahn 12 born, carrying every form at once<br>1885 &nbsp;his son's marriage enters the father under a short form reading Papa<br>after &nbsp;every descendant written Papić</span><br><br><strong>A by-name displaced a surname across three generations</strong>, which is an ordinary Croatian process, and there is now a document at three of the four steps.",
    f: "<strong>Two things would finish it.</strong> Ahn 12's own baptism at <strong>Selce, 11 March 1838</strong> — which will say which surname the priest gave him at the font, and is the one step of the four with no document. And the 1885 marriage at a readable size: the word there is at the limit of legibility on a 960-pixel scan, and it is the only place a contemporary hand names his surname in Croatian. <a href='/open-questions'>See 5c.</a><br><br><strong>And a guess, offered as one.</strong> Ahn 49, Margarita “Martha” Lončarić — the Martha of the 1806 entry — has a mother recorded as <strong>Anna Papić</strong>. A woman actually surnamed Papić marrying into this Antić household one generation before the by-name first appears is exactly how a nadimak arises: the household becomes known by the wife's family. It would explain where <em>Papa</em> came from. It is also a single undocumented name in a tree, and nothing here tests it — the <strong>Selce marriage of Pauli Lončarić and Anna Papić</strong> would." },

  { n: "5b", w: "high", t: "Two sisters born eighteen days apart",
    b: "<a href='/people/franka-papic'>Franjka Papić</a> and <a href='/people/sulka-papic'>Šulka Papić</a> are both daughters of <strong>Josip Papić and Antonija <em>r.</em> Prpić</strong> — Milka's parents, so both are <a href='/hedviga'>Hedviga's</a> aunts. Franjka's marriage register (Senj, 16 August 1914) gives her birth as <strong>20 January 1895</strong>. Šulka's (Senj, 21 December 1919) gives hers as <strong>7 February 1895</strong>. That is <strong>eighteen days</strong>, and they cannot both be right for full sisters.<br><br>Neither date is this archive's inference: each is written in that woman's own entry. A marriage register records <em>what the bride said</em>, not what the baptism book says, so either could be the loose one. They might be twins with garbled dates; one might belong to a different Papić couple, and there were at least three having children in Senj in these years.",
    f: "<strong>It is not a tree error.</strong> All four Papić sisters' marriage registers have now been read, and the tree reproduces every one of them exactly: <a href='/people/ana-papic'>Ana</a> 25 May 1888, <a href='/people/bozica-tonka-papic'>Božica</a> 9 December 1891, Franjka 20 January 1895, Šulka 7 February 1895. So the <em>registers</em> disagree with each other and the tree faithfully carries both — which moves the question off the transcription and onto the books.<br><br>The <strong>Senj baptism register for January–February 1895</strong> settles it. Both entries would fall within three weeks of each other in the same book, and it would take one sitting." },
  { n: "5c", w: "critical", t: "Four of the most important documents here are thumbnails",
    b: "The median scan in this archive is <strong>2,934 pixels</strong> wide and several are 4,500. <strong>Four are 960.</strong> They are not minor ones:<br><br><span class='mono'>1920 · Ljubo and Milka's marriage — the entry that answered question 1<br>1885 · Josip and Antonija's marriage — names four direct ancestors<br>1907 · Zvonimir and Milka's marriage — the hidden first marriage<br>1859 · Antonija Perpić's birth (a) — superseded by a 3,569px copy</span><br><br><strong>It is already costing readings.</strong> On the 1885 entry the groom's father's surname resolves only to a short form that looks like <em class='mono'>Papa</em>, with no visible <em>-ić</em> — which is the whole of <a href='/open-questions'>question 5</a>. And his bride's mother, <a href='/people/lucia'>Lucia</a>, is given a maiden name ending <em class='mono'>-pić</em> that <strong>cannot be read at all</strong>. She is one of the seven women on the direct line with no surname, and that one word would end it.<br><br>That the 1859 birth exists at 960px <em>and</em> at 3,569px shows the same event reached this archive twice by two different routes — and only one of them was any good.",
    f: "<strong>Corrected within the hour.</strong> This question first said the four had been <em>downloaded</em> at the wrong size and should be re-fetched. That was wrong, and testing it took two minutes: the MyHeritage CDN returns exactly 960 pixels for the 1885 entry, so <strong>the file held in the tree is itself 960 pixels</strong>. Nothing was lost in transit.<br><br><strong>What the good scans have in common is where they came from.</strong> Three documents here carry a <strong>FamilySearch image identifier</strong> in the filename — <span class='mono'>3QSQ-G99X-1QBW</span>, <span class='mono'>3QSQ-G99X-17PB</span>, <span class='mono'>3QSQ-G99X-174F</span> — and all three are <strong>4,500 pixels</strong>. Every 960-pixel file carries no such identifier. The big ones were pulled from FamilySearch; the small ones came by some poorer route and were uploaded to the tree as they were.<br><br>So the fix is to <strong>find these three entries on FamilySearch again</strong> and take them at full size. The registers and pages are already known: <strong>Senj marriages 8 December 1920</strong>; <strong>Senj marriages p.192 no.5</strong>, 3 May 1885; <strong>Senj marriages p.294 no.30</strong>, 4 November 1907. The fourth needs nothing — Antonija's 1859 birth already exists here at 3,569 pixels.<br><br><em>One known obstacle:</em> the FamilySearch image viewer will accept a page number but does not repaint its canvas under automation, so this may need a person at the keyboard rather than a script." },
  { n: "1f", w: "critical", t: "Twenty-two years, an emigration and a whole country",
    b: "<a href='/hedviga'>Hedviga</a> is at <strong>Senj in April 1953</strong>, when her son Robert is born. She is next seen arriving in <strong>South Africa in April 1975</strong>, and the entry says <em>“from Zimbabwe”</em>. <strong>Between those two dates this archive holds nothing whatever</strong> — not a departure, not an arrival, not an address. An entire country is known only from two words on a later record.<br><br><strong>The family remembers it as 1948</strong>, a post-war move for better and safer conditions. The record will not allow 1948: their second and third children were born at <strong>Senj in December 1951 and April 1953</strong>. But <strong>1948 is a real year here</strong> — Ladislava “Ada” was born at Crikvenica on 5 March 1948 — and a remembered date attaching itself to the wrong event is the commonest fault in this whole archive.<br><br>So the crossing happened <strong>after April 1953 and before 1973</strong>, when she is said to have married Santou Codron. Twenty years wide, and everything about the family's life in Rhodesia sits inside it — three children schooled somewhere, a husband working somewhere, and a marriage ending, since <strong>Ivan died back at Senj in 1995</strong>.",
    f: "<strong>A migration leaves more paper than a birth does</strong>, and none of it has been looked for, because until September 2026 this archive did not know there was a Rhodesian decade to look in.<br><br><span class='mono'>· Southern Rhodesia immigration and permit records, 1953–1965<br>· South African permanent-residence and naturalisation files, 1975<br>· Yugoslav passport applications — leaving was not a private act<br>· School enrolments in Salisbury or Bulawayo for three children<br>· Whatever ended the marriage, since he returned and she did not</span><br><br>The <strong>National Archives of Zimbabwe</strong> in Harare holds the immigration series. This is the largest single hole in the life of the person this archive exists for." },
  { n: 6, w: "medium", t: "Why is Antonija Prpić buried at Mirogoj?",
    b: "She was born at <strong>Krivi Put 136</strong> in 1859 and died at <strong>Senj</strong> in 1935 — but is buried at <strong>Mirogoj in Zagreb</strong>, 200 km away. Nothing in the record explains it. A child living in Zagreb is the obvious guess and it is only a guess.",
    f: "The <strong>Mirogoj burial register</strong>, which gives the grave, the date and who paid for it." },
  { n: 7, w: "medium", t: "Two deaths on 30 October, six years apart",
    b: "Ivan Anton Defranceski died at Senj on <strong>30 October 1995</strong>; Hedviga died at Johannesburg on <strong>30 October 2001</strong>. Both dates come from the tree, not from certificates seen here. A shared date is exactly what memory tidies into place.",
    f: "Both <strong>death certificates</strong> — Croatian civil registration for Senj 1995, South African for Johannesburg 2001." },
  { n: 8, w: "medium", t: "What was the Senj tobacco factory?",
    b: "Two women of this family worked there — <a href='/people/milka-lucia-papic'>Milka</a> as a supervisor, <strong>Anka Blažević</strong> (1893–1953) on the floor. The tree gives the building's address and says it is now a textile factory. Its founding date, size and closure are unconfirmed here; the machinery is said to have gone to <strong>Rovinj</strong> after the Second World War.",
    f: "The <strong>Senj town museum</strong> and Croatian industrial-history literature on the state tobacco monopoly after the patent of 20 November 1850.<br><br><strong>Its scale can now be read off one page.</strong> Senj marriages p.359 covers 20 November – 22 December 1919, entries 44 to 51. <strong>Six of the eight brides</strong> — Anka Blažević, Marija Ilić, Ivka Babić, Marija Babić, Božica Zrinski and <a href='/people/sulka-papic'>Šulka Papić</a> — are entered <em>radnica u tvornici duhana</em>, and entry 45's witness Ivan Antić is a <em>mašinista</em> in it.<br><br>Three pages across three registers, counted the same way:<br><br><span class='mono'>p.303 · 1909 · 3 of 5 brides<br>p.350 · 1918 · 3 of 6 brides<br>p.359 · 1919 · 6 of 8 brides</span><br><br>This is not one unusual page. Across at least 1909–1919 the factory is the standing occupation of the young women marrying in Senj, and men appear in it too — <strong>Mate Tadić</strong>, <em>radnik u tvornici duhana</em>, and <strong>Ivan Antić</strong>, <em>mašinista</em>.<br><br><strong>Ten pages have now been counted the same way</strong>, 1909 to 1919: 3 of 5 brides, 3 of 5, 4 of 5, 4 of 5, 4 of 5, 3 of 4, 3 of 6, <strong>7 of 8</strong>, 6 of 8. It is not one unusual page or one unusual year.<br><br><strong>And the register shows the whole hierarchy</strong>, because it records each person's grade:<br><br><span class='mono'>radnica / radnik &nbsp;&nbsp;worker<br>vratar &nbsp;&nbsp;doorman<br>mašinista &nbsp;&nbsp;machinist<br>mestar &nbsp;&nbsp;master<br>nadglednica / nadglednik &nbsp;&nbsp;supervisor<br>nadčinovnik &nbsp;&nbsp;senior clerk</span><br><br><a href='/people/milka-lucia-papic'>Milka</a> is a <em>nadglednica</em>; so is <a href='/people/antonija-blazevic'>Antonija Blažević</a>. Two women of this family reached supervisor, which makes the factory a career and not merely a wage.<br><br><strong>It was also a route out.</strong> <a href='/people/antonija-blazevic-2'>Antonija “Tonka” Blažević</a> married <strong>Ginro Babić</strong> in 1912 — <em>nadglednik u tvornici duhana <strong>na Rieci</strong></em> — and went to Rijeka with him; the witness at that wedding, Andrija Babić, held the same grade at the same Rijeka factory. Floor in Senj, supervisor in Rijeka." },
  { n: 9, w: "data", t: "Three place errors that will mislead someone",
    b: "<strong>(a)</strong> Juraj Blažević's birthplace is geocoded to <em>Smokvica, Općina Smokvica, Dubrovačko-neretvanska županija</em> — that is <strong>Smokvica on Korčula</strong>, 400 km south. The village meant is <strong>Smokvica Krmpotska</strong> near Novi Vinodolski. <strong>(b)</strong> Ivan Žubrinić of Port Pirie is given <em>“Senj, Split-Dalmatia”</em>; Senj is in <strong>Lika-Senj</strong>. <strong>(c)</strong> A FamilySearch citation for Nicolaus Žubrinić's Otočac christening is catalogued under <em>“Osekovo, Belovár-Körös Megye, Hungary”</em>.",
    f: "Correcting (a) and (b) in the MyHeritage tree at source. (c) is FamilySearch's error, not the tree's — but it should be noted wherever the citation is reused." },
  { n: 10, w: "answered", t: "The photographs are not in the export — ANSWERED",
    b: "The GEDCOM contains no media records at all. <strong>Seventy items</strong> have now been swept out of the MyHeritage media library across the whole line — 34 of them register scans, certificates and gravestones, many at 4,500 px. Three are read out in full: <a href='/marriage-1920'>the 1920 marriage register</a>, <a href='/certificate'>the 1951 birth extract</a>, <a href='/grave'>the family grave</a>. <a href='/pictures'>All of them →</a>",
    f: "Nothing further for the direct line. The same sweep has <strong>not</strong> been run on the wider register, and the site library holds about <strong>4,500 items</strong> in total." },
  { n: 11, w: "high", t: "Who is in the photographs?",
    b: "Most of the recovered photographs carry <strong>no caption but a camera filename</strong> — <em>1595624858.9990501</em>, <em>1134835622</em>, <em>IMG_5752</em>. Faces this archive can name are guesses from resemblance and from the ages of children in shot. <a href='/pictures'>The pictures →</a>",
    f: "Somebody in the family looking at them. This is the one open question here that <strong>no archive can close</strong> and a single phone call could — so the pictures have been gathered onto <a href='/faces'>a single contact sheet</a> meant to be put in front of a relative, with what is being asked of each one spelled out.<br><br><strong>And five of them were not photographs.</strong> Five items were being published as portraits of ancestors born between 1806 and 1838; they are stock question-mark placeholder graphics, one with a visible Adobe Stock watermark. They are now excluded from the build. <a href='/faces'>The five →</a>" },
  { n: 12, w: "answered", t: "Why does Ivan Defranceski have Arolsen card files? — ANSWERED",
    b: "They are his, beyond reasonable doubt: all three give the birth as <strong>25 June 1925 at Crikvenica</strong>, matching the <a href='/certificate'>1951 civil birth extract</a> exactly. They place him at <strong>Licht und Kraft, Reutlingen</strong>, as an <em>Hilfsarbeiter</em> — unskilled labourer — from <strong>21 October 1941, aged sixteen</strong>, lodging at Brunnenstraße 7, Pfullingen, with a second Reutlingen employer from January 1942. <a href='/arolsen'>The full reading →</a>",
    f: "Nothing further on identity. <strong>What replaces it is 12b and 12c.</strong>" },
  { n: "12b", w: "critical", t: "Was he a forced labourer or a free one?",
    b: "The 1950 municipal questionnaires carry three printed lines designed to answer exactly this — <em>Wurde der Arbeiter restlos entlohnt?</em> (was he paid in full), <em>Dienstverpflichtet</em> (compulsorily assigned), <em>Freier Arbeiter … seit</em> (free worker, since). <strong>All three are blank on both cards.</strong> The collection is titled a card file of <em>persecutees</em>, but that is the name of a zone-wide registration file, not a finding about anyone in it. <strong>This archive claims neither.</strong>",
    f: "A direct enquiry to the <strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> for the full file on Defranceski, b. 25.6.1925 — they answer families without charge, and the online collections may hold employment or transport records these three cards were extracted from." },
  { n: "12c", w: "high", t: "How long was he in Germany?",
    b: "The second employment runs from <strong>26 January 1942</strong> to <strong>28 November 194?</strong> — the last digit of the year runs into the column rule and cannot be read. <strong>1942 gives ten months in Germany; 1944 gives just over three years.</strong> His daughter is born at Crikvenica in 1948, so he was home by then, and nothing at all is documented between the card and that birth.",
    f: "A higher-resolution scan of the insurance card, or the Arolsen file itself. The second employer's name is also unread — an old German hand, possibly beginning <em>Otto</em>." },
  { n: 13, w: "data", t: "The gravestone contradicts the register",
    b: "The <a href='/grave'>Senj stone</a> gives Ljubomir's birth as <strong>1891</strong>. The <a href='/marriage-1920'>1920 marriage register</a>, written in his lifetime from his baptismal certificate, gives <strong>1 March 1892</strong>. This archive follows the register.",
    f: "The <strong>Rijeka baptismal entry of 1892</strong> would settle it beyond argument — and would also independently name his parents a second time." },
];
const cls = { critical: "warn", high: "", medium: "", data: "dim" };
---
<Base title="Open Questions"
  eyebrow={`${Q.filter((q) => q.w !== "answered").length} open · ${Q.filter((q) => q.w === "answered").length} answered`}
  dek="Written down so they can be worked on, and so no reader mistakes a gap for a fact. Each one says what would actually close it."
>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The most valuable thing anyone can send this archive is a
    <strong>correction</strong>. The second most valuable is a document that closes one of
    these.</p>
  </section>

  {Q.map((q) => (
    <section class="blk qa">
      <div class="lab">{q.w === "answered" ? "Answered — kept so the change is visible"
        : q.w === "critical" ? "Critical — the spine depends on it"
        : q.w === "data" ? "Data quality" : q.w === "high" ? "Substantive" : "Worth doing"}</div>
      <h2><span class="mono dim">{q.n}.</span> {q.t}</h2>
      <p set:html={base(q.b)} />
      <div class="note"><strong>What would settle it: </strong>
        <span set:html={base(q.f)} /></div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Drafted, not sent</div>
    <h2>Three letters</h2>
    <p class="lede">Three of these questions can only be answered by a book that is not online. The
    enquiries are written and sit in <code class="mono">requests/</code>, each naming exactly what
    is wanted and what is already known, so the search can be narrow. <strong>None has been
    sent.</strong></p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Rijeka,<br />Senj branch</th><td><strong>The registers of Sv. Jakov, Krmpote</strong> —
        the parish named by Toma's 1907 death entry, and therefore the book holding every Blažević
        above him. Wanted: <strong>Jurko's baptism</strong> (likely 1855–1870), his
        <strong>marriage to Tereza Žubrinić</strong> (likely 1885–92), or his <strong>death</strong>
        before December 1920. Any one of the three names his father and ends
        <a href={u("/open-questions")}>question 1b</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Rijeka,<br />Senj branch</th><td><strong>Senj, 1915 — the death of Zvonimir
        Vukelić</strong>: how Milka came to be widowed at twenty-eight, about which this archive
        knows nothing at all.</td></tr>
      <tr><th><strong>Gospić</strong><br /><span class="dim">added 10 Sept 2026</span></th>
        <td><strong>Otočac — one man called Michaël Žubrinić, or two?</strong> The 1834 baptism at
        Šumećica 24 and the 1880 son named at his 1910 marriage give parents of the
        <em>same two names</em> forty-six years apart. Wanted: a
        <strong>Žubrinić–Orešković marriage before 1834</strong>, and whether a
        <strong>second one exists around 1855–70</strong>. One entry, or its absence, decides four
        generations. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Question 4d</a>.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Two of the three were first addressed to the
    wrong archive.</strong> Gospić covers Lika-Senj <em>“osim gradova Senja i Novalje”</em> —
    except the towns of Senj and Novalja — and Smokvica Krmpotska is not in Lika-Senj at all.
    Senj, Krmpote and Krivi Put belong to <strong>Rijeka</strong>; Otočac and Karlobag to
    <strong>Gospić</strong>. <a href={u("/changed")}>That correction →</a></p>
    <p class="dek">All three drafts say plainly that <strong>a nil return is worth having</strong>
    and will be recorded as one. An archive that holds nothing is a finding; an archive never asked
    is not.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the standing one</h2>
    <p class="note">Everything above generation eight is <strong>silence</strong>, not mystery. The
    Croatian parish registers for this coast begin in the sixteenth century and many survive; the
    Blažević line simply has not been followed into them below <strong>Mateša Blažević, born about
    1750</strong>. That is a research gap, and it is closable by someone with the patience to read
    Latin parish hands. <a href={u("/how-far-back")}>How far back →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
